  "worked_solution": "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$",
